## maximum queue times and run times
## these times are in hours *since last status update*
## status updates generally happen somewhat frequently
## if UDP messaging from the cluster isn't working, then status updates will be missed
## set to zero to disable, which is the default: a job is left for its submitter
## to cancel, since a missed status update is not evidence of a hang

$global_max_queue_time_hours = 0;
$global_max_run_time_hours   = 0;

## abandonment ceiling, in hours since last status update.
##
## While a cluster is unreachable gridctl defers the stall timers above, and
## keeps retrying a finished job's results. This caps both: past this many
## hours the job is closed out as failed. With the stall timers at zero it
## only limits the result retries. Zero defers indefinitely.
##
## Make it comfortably longer than a routine maintenance window: it should
## catch a site that is gone, not a site that is down for the afternoon.
## cluster_probe_outage_verdict() in gridctl explains the policy.
$global_cluster_abandon_hours = 72;

## sbatch retries (round 8: narrowed back from "any definite verdict"), when
## ssh never ran the command at all (connect failure or an open breaker) or
## sbatch's own final line says it never reached the controller (a pre-send
## connect failure, distinct from ssh's own). Every other definite verdict --
## a real rejection reason (invalid partition, account/partition, time
## limit, node configuration, QOS/accounting policy) or sbatch's own
## busy/EAGAIN give-up after its internal retry budget -- is reported
## without a retry of ours: a rejection reason will not change on retry, and
## sbatch has already spent up to ~120s retrying the busy case internally
## before printing that verdict. An sbatch whose outcome is merely unknown --
## it started, and the connection was then lost or timed out before its
## answer came back -- is never repeated either way, since the job may
## already exist. Per-cluster 'submit_retries' / 'submit_retry_wait' override
## these.
$global_sbatch_submit_retries            = 3;
$global_sbatch_submit_retry_wait_seconds = 5;

## sbatch's own command timeout, longer than remote_exec's 120s default
## (round 8): sbatch answers EAGAIN by retrying internally for up to
## ~120s/15 attempts before printing its own busy verdict (measured at
## ~121.4s), so the 120s default could kill it a fraction of a second
## before that verdict printed, reading as "cluster unreachable (TIMED_OUT)
## ... outcome unknown" for a call that was about to give a clean, definite
## answer instead. Per-cluster 'sbatch_timeout_seconds' overrides this.
$global_sbatch_timeout_seconds = 150;

## after a job reports COMPLETE, jobmonitor waits for the cluster's (UDP)
## "Finished" message before finalizing.  Because UDP is unreliable, it will
## finalize anyway once $global_complete_grace_seconds have elapsed since the
## job was first seen COMPLETE (measured on the jobmonitor's own clock, so it
## is immune to timezone/clock skew between the LIMS host, cluster, and DB).
## $global_complete_max_seconds is a hard ceiling: a job stuck COMPLETE past
## this is force-finalized.  If unset, jobmonitor defaults to 600 and 21600.

$global_complete_grace_seconds = 600;
$global_complete_max_seconds   = 21600;

## sbatch is retried when it provably never started (the command's begin
## marker never came back, as with a connect failure or an open breaker) or
## when sbatch's own final line says it never reached the controller at all.
## Every other definite verdict sbatch can print -- a real rejection reason,
## or its own busy/EAGAIN give-up -- is reported without a retry of ours
## (round 8; see $global_sbatch_submit_retries above for why). An SSH
## failure or dropped connection after the begin marker, with no definite
## verdict line to go on, can mean Slurm accepted the job while its ID was
## lost on the way back, and retrying then would risk a duplicate, so that
## case is left for an operator to reconcile on the scheduler.
## $global_sbatch_submit_retries and $global_sbatch_submit_retry_wait_seconds set
## the budget; unset, the code uses 4 attempts total (the default retries, 3,
## plus the first), waiting 5s/10s/20s between them -- the wait doubles each
## time, it is not a constant interval.

## Remote execution budgets, retries and breaker policy are defined in
## class/remote_exec.php. GNU timeout is required for wall-clock limits.

## Maximum age of cluster health records in seconds. Stale records are treated
## as down, and so is a cluster with no record at all. cluster_status.php runs
## every 12 minutes, so this wants to be at least two probe intervals or one slow
## probe marks a healthy cluster down. Zero disables the age check. Unset, the
## code uses 1800, which is what this matches.
$global_cluster_status_max_age_seconds = 1800;


## Optional breaker directory; defaults to ~us3/lims/etc/circuit-breaker.
## Provision it as 2770 us3:<web group> so both the web tier and the us3 daemons
## can create their own state directory inside it. It must be owned by us3 (or
## root) and not be a symlink, or the breaker is disabled. Not under /var/tmp:
## PrivateTmp splits it per service.
##
## Each account keeps its own state in a 0700 subdirectory with 0600 files, so a
## file deciding whether a cluster is contacted is never writable by another
## account. A breaker tripped by the web tier therefore does not back off the
## daemons, and the other way round.

